<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!-- Served by an *application instance*, not by a daemon. Bus name:
     org.legeos.Instance.i<InstanceId compact> (the instance claims it for itself at startup —
     see lege_types::dbus::checkpoint). Path: /org/legeos/Checkpoint.

     Every other org.legeos.* interface is a daemon answering clients; this one is inverted.
     lege-resourced decides that pressure needs containment, lege-sessiond relays the request
     here, and the application answers. That is what makes automatic termination survivable:
     "freezing an application is not enough — it stops CPU use but does not release its
     anonymous memory" (planning doc 01 section 5), so an application that can save and be
     restarted is worth far more to the governor than one that can only be killed.

     Legacy applications simply do not serve this, and are governed with less confidence: no
     unsaved-work signal, no restore. That is a supported outcome, not a failure.

     The request and response are the JSON encodings of
     lege_types::checkpoint::{CheckpointRequest, CheckpointResponse} — one method rather than
     one per message, so the wire format and the documented protocol cannot drift apart:

       prepare-for-pressure  -> prepared { released_bytes }
       checkpoint-now        -> checkpoint-complete { token, has_unsaved_work } | refused { reason }
       restore-instance      -> checkpoint-complete { token } | refused { reason }

     A `refused` answer is a legitimate reply (the instance is mid-transaction and cannot save
     right now), not an error — and the governor may still terminate after the deadline. A D-Bus
     error means something else went wrong entirely.

     The session daemon must verify the peer before trusting an answer: resolve the name's PID
     to its cgroup, exactly as AppIdentity1 resolves a window. A bus name is a claim. -->
<node>
  <interface name="org.legeos.Checkpoint1">
    <method name="Request">
      <arg name="request_json" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="response_json" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
  </interface>
</node>
